<header><p class="st-kicker">sultanate · Maritime Southeast Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Palembang Sultanate</h1><p class="st-meta">1659 CE – 1823 CE · capital Palembang</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Javanese-influenced Malay sultanate of south Sumatra that grew rich on pepper and Bangka tin until Dutch conquest in 1821.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a> had long been ruled by princes of Javanese descent loyal to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/mataram-sultanate">Mataram</a>. In 1659 the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dutch-east-india-company-territories">Dutch East India Company</a> burned the town after its envoys were killed, and the ruler Kimas Hindi broke with Mataram and proclaimed himself sultan with the title Abdurrahman, founding an independent sultanate on the Musi river.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The court combined Javanese ceremony with Malay <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a> sold <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pepper">pepper</a> to the Company under monopoly contracts, and after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a> was found on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bangka">Bangka</a> around 1710 it became one of the richest states of the archipelago. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-badaruddin-i">Mahmud Badaruddin I</a> built the Great Mosque, and a later sultan built the brick kraton of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kuto-besak">Kuto Besak</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sultan">Sultan</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahmud-badaruddin-ii">Mahmud Badaruddin II</a> resisted both the British during <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/british-java">British Java</a> and the returning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a>, defeating a Dutch fleet in 1819. A larger expedition took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palembang">Palembang</a> in 1821, the sultan was exiled to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ternate-sultanate">Ternate</a>, and the sultanate was abolished in 1823. He is now an Indonesian national hero.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/palembang-sultanate.jpg" alt="Palembang Sultanate" width="320" height="203"><figcaption>Image: Syazwi Irfan · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Old_Flag_of_Sultanate_of_Palembang.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1659 CE – 1823 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>sultanate</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Palembang</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Abdurrahman, Mahmud Badaruddin I, Mahmud Badaruddin II</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Sunni Islam</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Palembang Malay</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/indonesia">Indonesia</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Palembang Darussalam, Sultanate of Palembang</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1741&amp;focus=palembang-sultanate" aria-label="Palembang Sultanate on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7588 2289 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Palembang Sultanate lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7908 2545L8052 2725M7908 2545L7959 2663"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M8052 2725h0M7959 2663h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7908 2545h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7908 2545h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Palembang. <a href="/atlas?year=1741&amp;focus=palembang-sultanate">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
